# Collector v3 cron jobs (pollers run under systemd, NOT cron).
CRON_TZ=Asia/Tokyo
# 5-minute offsets are generous, not exact: static-fetch.sh/rotate-day.sh
# write via mktemp+atomic mv, so even a slow run overlapping the next job
# never exposes a partial file to sync-r2.sh's include filters -- worst
# case is one file syncing a day late, never corruption or loss.
0  9 * * *  nice -n 10 /home/opc/collector/bin/static-fetch.sh >> /home/opc/collector/cron.log 2>&1
5  9 * * *  nice -n 10 /home/opc/collector/bin/rotate-day.sh  >> /home/opc/collector/cron.log 2>&1
10 9 * * *  nice -n 10 /home/opc/collector/bin/sync-r2.sh     >> /home/opc/collector/cron.log 2>&1
# prune.sh REFUSES to run (exit 65) unless sync-r2.sh's .sync-r2.last-ok
# marker is fresh (see both scripts) -- replaces the old "workstation
# mirror" (this repo's make fetch + make sync-r2) as prune's actual,
# enforced parity gate, not just a documented assumption. Still: nothing
# pages on either script's nonzero exit yet, so a real R2 outage shows up
# as prune.sh silently skipping its weekly run (visible in cron.log) until
# someone looks -- wiring both into the existing healthchecks.io ping_url
# convention (see agencies.tsv) would close that last gap.
30 9 * * 0 /home/opc/collector/bin/prune.sh                   >> /home/opc/collector/cron.log 2>&1
